  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery concentrates more probability on favorable outcomes (i.e., less negative ones), which would be Lottery A (99.9% chance of 0 vs. 99.8% in B), making it more left-skewed. The individual, however, does not consider the higher probability of the best outcome (0) in A as an advantage. Instead, they are deterred by the size of the -6000 outcome and dismiss the 0.1% probability difference as insufficient to compensate for the larger loss. Their reasoning focuses on loss aversion and perceived risk severity, not on the asymmetry or skewness of the outcome distribution. There is no mention of how probabilities are distributed across outcomes in a way that reflects skewness. Therefore, the individual’s justification does not align with the skewness-based rule. The information considered (magnitude of loss, probability difference) overlaps partially with the inputs to skewness but is interpreted through a different cognitive lens.",
